Both pass. Now DOM a11y `aria-setsize`/`aria-posinset` on windowed rows. The SSR HTML renderer emits rows without knowing their absolute index (the row nodes don't carry the index). The lazy container has `FIRST_ITEM_INDEX`; the renderer could add `aria-setsize`/`aria-posinset` to the window rows. But the rows are arbitrary subtrees; the row root is the child node. The HTML renderer can, when rendering a lazy stack's child at index `i`, add attributes to the child's root element. The `render_node` function renders children generically; to add per-child attributes, we'd need to pass the absolute index to `render_node` or post-process. This is a larger change. The DOM client can add `aria-setsize`/`aria-posinset` after hydration based on the lazy element's attributes and child order. But the rows may be nested; the direct children of the lazy element are the row roots. We can set:
```js
el.setAttribute("role", "list");
for each child: child.setAttribute("role","listitem"); child.setAttribute("aria-setsize", count); child.setAttribute("aria-posinset", first + i + 1)
```
in `refreshLazyPadding` or `refreshLazyWindows`. This is simple and improves a11y for the live DOM. Add to `refreshLazyPadding`:
```js
const rows = Array.from(el.children).filter(...);
rows.forEach((row, i) => {
  row.setAttribute("aria-setsize", String(count));
  row.setAttribute("aria-posinset", String(first + i + 1));
});
el.setAttribute("role", el.getAttribute("role") ?? "list");
```
But setting `role="list"` on the lazy element may conflict with the app's `ROLE` property. If the app sets a role, don't override. Use `if (!el.hasAttribute("role")) el.setAttribute("role","list")`. And `listitem` on rows only if no role. This is a nice touch. Let me add it to `refreshLazyPadding`.

Also, the SSR should ideally emit these for the first paint. The server renderer could add `role="list"` to the lazy wrapper and `aria-setsize`/`aria-posinset` to the rows. The row root elements are rendered by `render_node`; we can post-process the children string? Not easily. The DOM client adds them after hydration; acceptable for v1.

Let me add to `refreshLazyPadding`:
```ts
const rows = Array.from(el.children).filter(
  (c): c is HTMLElement => c instanceof HTMLElement && c.hasAttribute("data-pathland-id"),
);
if (!el.hasAttribute("role")) el.setAttribute("role", "list");
rows.forEach((row, i) => {
  if (!row.hasAttribute("role")) row.setAttribute("role", "listitem");
  row.setAttribute("aria-setsize", String(count));
  row.setAttribute("aria-posinset", String(first + i + 1));
});
```
- The `first` is the absolute first index of the window; `i` is the row position. Good.
- The rows may include non-row children (e.g. a header) but v1 requires a single ForEach. Fine.
- Add to the status note (a11y implemented). Let me do it and run the TS tests/typecheck.

